      "card_ids": ["C21"],
      "claim": "Ferulic acid, a phenolic carboxylic acid, shows only 3-10% oral bioavailability in humans, consistent with the query's multiple carboxylic acids and poor permeability.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C40"],
      "claim": "Tegaserod, a polar ionizable drug, has an absolute oral bioavailability of approximately 10% in healthy volunteers, supporting low F for the polar, ionizable query.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C08"],
      "claim": "Tetrahydrouridine, a highly polar molecule with many H-bond donors/acceptors, has a human oral bioavailability of only 4.1%, analogous to the query's high polarity and likely poor absorption.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C51"],
      "claim": "The direct administration of oseltamivir carboxylate, a carboxylic acid-containing molecule, results in poor oral bioavailability (<5%) in humans, relevant to the query's free carboxylic acid groups.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "Predicted low oral bioavailability (F < 20%) based on the query's polar dicarboxylic acid and diketopiperazine structure, consistent with low-F analogues such as acamprosate, ferulic acid, tegaserod, and tetrahydrouridine.",
